## TL;DR  

*The current heuristic (`text-anchor` missing **and** `symbol-placement === "point"`) is **not reliable** for spotting route-shield layers.*  
*Shield layers are identified by the **icon-image** they use (a placeholder that resolves to a badge image) and by a few other layout keys that are *usually* present:*

| Layout key | Typical value for a shield | Why it matters |
|------------|----------------------------|----------------|
| `icon-image` | an **expression** that contains a placeholder such as `"{network}_{ref_length}"` or `"{ref}_shield"` (or a prefixed id like `"my-icons:us-interstate_2"`). | The badge image is the only thing that can be missing; the text (the route number) is supplied by `text-field`. |
| `text-field` | `"{ref}"` (or any property that holds the route number). | Shields always have a numeric label. |
| `icon-text-fit` | `"both"` / `"width"` (or at least **not** `"none"`). | Makes the badge stretch to fit the number when the style uses a *single* badge image for many lengths. |
| `icon-text-fit-padding` | optional array of numbers. | Present when the badge is stretchable. |
| `symbol-placement` | **either** `"point"` **or** a **zoom-function** object that switches between `"point"` and `"line"` (e.g. `{ "base": 1, "stops": [[7,"point"],[8,"line"]] }`). | The presence of a function is a strong clue that the layer is a shield (most OSM-based styles do this). |
| `icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-overlap` | Usually `false` (default) – shields behave like normal symbols. | Not a discriminator, but worth keeping for completeness. |
| `text-anchor` | **usually** `"center"` (the default) – it is *defined* in the style spec, but many styles omit it, so its absence is **not** a reliable signal. | The original heuristic relied on this, but the spec says the default is `"center"`; the property may be omitted and still be a shield. |

Because **`text-anchor` is optional** and many shield layers *do* omit it, checking for its absence will flag any point-symbol layer that does not explicitly set a text anchor – which includes the huge majority of place-name layers. Likewise, many place-name layers also use `symbol-placement: "point"` (or a zoom-function that resolves to `"point"` at low zoom), so the two-condition test is too broad.

---

## Why the current heuristic fails

| Condition | What it actually catches | Why it’s a false-positive for place-names |
|-----------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined` | *Any* symbol layer that does not explicitly set a text-anchor (the default is `"center"`). | Most place-name layers never set `text-anchor`. |
| `layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'` | Layers that render symbols as points (including point-only labels, POI icons, etc.). | Shield layers also render as points at low zoom, but many non-shield layers do the same. |
| **Both together** | The intersection of the two sets above – still a *very large* set of layers, most of which are not shields. | Hence the heuristic tags plain place-name layers as shields and misses many real shields that use a zoom-function for `symbol-placement` or that explicitly set `text-anchor`. |

In short: **absence of `text-anchor` is not a shield flag**, and **`symbol-placement: "point"` is not shield-specific**.

---

## A more robust heuristic

Below is a **step-by-step decision tree** you can embed in your audit script. It works for the two reference styles (OSM Bright & OSM Liberty) and for most custom styles that follow the same pattern.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a layer looks like a route-shield layer.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a style layer object (already parsed)
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer with an icon and a text field.
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;
  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 2️⃣ Must have a text-field (the route number). If missing → not a shield.
  if (!layout['text-field']) return false;

  // 3️⃣ The icon-image must be *dynamic* – i.e. an expression that resolves
  //    to a sprite name based on feature properties.
  const iconImg = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!iconImg) return false;

  // Helper: is the value a string that contains a placeholder?
  const containsPlaceholder = (str) => /\{[^}]+\}/.test(str);

  // a) Plain string with a placeholder (e.g. "{network}_{ref_length}")
  // b) An expression array that eventually resolves to a string with a placeholder
  //    (most styles use a simple string, but we handle the array case for completeness)
  const isDynamicIcon =
    typeof iconImg === 'string' && containsPlaceholder(iconImg) ||
    Array.isArray(iconImg) && iconImg.some(
      (part) => typeof part === 'string' && containsPlaceholder(part)
    );

  if (!isDynamicIcon) return false;

  // 4️⃣ Optional but strong signal: icon-text-fit is NOT "none"
  //    (stretchable badge or badge-per-ref-length)
  const textFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  if (textFit && textFit !== 'none') return true; // we already have a shield

  // 5️⃣ If we haven't returned yet, look at symbol-placement.
  //    Shield layers often use a zoom-function that switches between point & line.
  const placement = layout['symbol-placement'];
  const isZoomFunc = placement && typeof placement === 'object' && 'stops' in placement;
  if (isZoomFunc) {
    // Check that one of the stops resolves to "point"
    const hasPoint = placement.stops.some(([_, val]) => val === 'point');
    if (hasPoint) return true;
  }

  // 6️⃣ Fallback: if the icon-image references a known shield-prefix (e.g. "us-", "road-")
  //    you can maintain a small whitelist of prefixes that are typical for shields.
  const knownPrefixes = ['us-', 'road-', 'default-'];
  if (typeof iconImg === 'string') {
    const baseName = iconImg.split(':').pop(); // strip possible sprite-sheet prefix
    if (knownPrefixes.some(p => baseName.startsWith(p))) return true;
  }

  // If none of the above matched, treat it as a normal point symbol.
  return false;
}
```

### Why this works

| Heuristic step | What it catches | Why it’s reliable |
|----------------|----------------|-------------------|
| **`text-field` present** | All shield layers need a numeric label (`{ref}` or similar). | Place-name layers *also* have a text-field, but they **don’t** pair it with a dynamic badge. |
| **`icon-image` contains a placeholder** (`{…}`) | The badge image is chosen from the sprite based on feature properties (`network`, `ref_length`, etc.). | Ordinary POI icons are static strings (`"cafe"`, `"park"`). |
| **`icon-text-fit` not `"none"`** | Indicates a *stretchable* badge (single image used for many ref lengths). | Most non-shield symbols never set `icon-text-fit`. |
| **`symbol-placement` is a zoom-function that includes `"point"`** | The OSM-based shield layers switch to line placement at higher zooms; the presence of a function is a strong shield indicator. | Plain place-name layers usually have a plain string (`"point"`), not a function. |
| **Known badge-prefix whitelist** | Catches styles that use a static badge name (`"us-interstate_2"` etc.) without a placeholder. | The whitelist can be extended per-project; it’s a safety net for styles that hard-code badge names. |

If you want **zero false-positives**, you can require **both** a placeholder *and* either `icon-text-fit !== "none"` **or** a zoom-function for `symbol-placement`. That will exclude any layer that merely uses a placeholder for something else (rare) and will still capture the classic shield layers.

